This strategy succeeds quite regularly for me (that
far, _my_ survival problems even when playing
alertly seem to become insurmountable these days
around DLVL 11).

[People call this "the protection racket", sometimes
disparagingly, but even if you NEVER intend to buy
protection, getting a good armor-based AC built up
from stuff found in Mines, _before_ your PC starts
taking on tougher monsters, is worlds easier with
this approach. Wimpy monsters drop great armor
fairly often, so there's no real need to provoke the
RNG to generating tough monsters early in the game.]

Remember the monster strength rule: it's based on
the average of the DLVL and your XL.

If your XL goes up, so do the randomly generated
monsters' HDs.

If your _pets'_ HDs go up, this has no effect on the
monster HD of generated monsters.

Obvious conclusion: spend early levels buffing up
your pet, who will become a big monster while the
enemy monsters remain wimps.

Keep your own XPs as close to zero as humanly (or
orcishly, gnomishly, elvishly, dwarvishly, et
cetera) possible, to help your pet by keeping enemy
strength low.

Run away from _every_ combat, lead the monster to
your pet, to be killed by your pet instead of you.

This means you need to keep carefully close to your
pet as you explore each level. Let your pet lead, or
herd it down the passages by blocking its path back.

Dance circles 'round your pet during the combat, so
you'll be close to the monster corpse when it dies.
Grab and eat every corpse you can get to before your
pet eats it, to prolong your survival.

Bumping your pet to displace it from a corpse it
hasn't grabbed yet but will beat you to otherwise
sometimes (rarely, but still worth trying) works.

If playing a zero starting alingment PC class, kill,
as late as possible, _one_, one XP monster, to get a
bit of positive alignment, so prayers to your deity
for food will be answered usefully.

Explore every level _thoroughly_, so your pet has
time to buff up sufficiently by killing easy
monsters. Remember, even with your XL staying at
one, the monster HDs go up as DLVL/2, so let your
pet earn lots of kills (and thus HP) to stay well
ahead of the expected monster HD levels for each
level you explore.

[The reason this whole plan only works roughly to
the entrance level of Sokoban is that your cat or
dog, unpolymorphed, is limited to HPs the equivalent
of a 9 HD monster, and much deeper, even if your XL
were still 1, the DLVL/2 component starts producing
monsters that can easily kill a 9 HD pet.  Multiple
pets help a bit, to absorb part of the damage during
a battle for each other, but after a while, the
monsters produced are so powerful your pet refuses
to mess with them.  See below for ways to cope with
this issue once you have bought protection.]

Even if the entrance to Mines is at level 2, explore
all of DLVL 1-4 before entering Mines, again, the
idea is that you want to hit Mines, and its clusters
of monsters, with, ideally, at least one 9 HD pet at
your side.

Once you do enter Mines, stick to the stairs until
the initial glut of monsters is taken out by your
pet, then explore slowly and carefully in case other
groups of monsters were cut off from the stairs.
Remember to retreat to hallways as soon as enemy
forces have been located, so your pet can take them
one by one and block their melee access to you.
Retreating while letting your pet retreat with you,
to a corner where you can hide from missile weapons
and wands, is also useful.

While stairs-sticking, you'll often get hit while
your pet is doing the killing, so pop upstairs to
rebuild your HPs as needed.

A useful thing to do with this recovery time,
instead of just resting, is to go (if you managed to
get your pet back up the stairs with you) to drag
all the worthless cruft you abandoned around the
level above, drop it on the stairs, several times if
needed until it falls to the (Mines) level below,
and thus move it closer to being salable to a Mines
Town shopkeeper, 

Only step away from the stairs to grab "stuff"
dropped or death dropped by monsters killed by your
pet, when you have plenty of visibility to assure
you have running room to reach the stairs before the
monsters reach you. With usual luck, you'll find
lots of goodies to improve your AC, brought right to
your feet and dropped there by helpful, and
helpfully dead, monsters, and your pet will BUC-test
them for you, while this process continues. Trying
them on upstairs so you don't get caught by a
monster swarm during a long dressing interval is a
good, cautious thing to do, and you can stack the
rejects by the stairs and so out of reach of the
monsters below.

The moment you score a pick axe (preferably, for its
lighter burden) or a dwarven mattock, give up the
clearing of Mines for a while, go back and clean out
any gold in vaults or just sitting around in the
rocky areas of DLVL 1-4. If you're not a credit
cloner, or haven't found a shopkeeper to bilk of
his/her assets, this will give you some boodle for
bribing the Mines Town priest.

Even though you're nowhere close to thinking about
your ascension run yet, it doesn't hurt, once a
Mines level is cleared of its initial monster cadre,
to burrow a direct path from the down stairs back to
the up stairs before descending to the next level.
That gives you a surefire way to _find_ the up
stairs if amnesia strikes, lets your pet have a few
more turns at comparatively easy monsters, and makes
sure your HP are topped off before descending. You
can also use this path to transport cruft to be
dropped down another level.

Really conservative play would find you creating
multiple connections between Mines level cul-de-sacs
so your stupid pet, whose PC finding algorithm lacks
power, doesn't get stuck where it has no algorithmic
way to get to where you are.  [This works for the
normal DLVLs too; add connectivity to any room where
the doors are not in the middle of the wall or the
room is long and skinny enough to constitute a
cul-de-sac to one side of an entranceway or the room
only has one entrance. IIUC, your pet, when trying
to find you, will only explore paths that go five
steps in a direction away from you before turning
back toward you. Many times this is insufficient.]

Eventually, this plan will find you in Mines Town
with one or more 9 HD pets, money to bribe the
priest for protection, and a "what do I do now"
attitude.

My suggestion is to take your new-found excellent
AC, your buffed up pet, and your weenie XL all the
way back to DLVL 1, and break your near pacifist
conduct there, only descending at a rate that finds
you well in charge against the strengths of the
monsters being generated.  An alternative is to go
clear out Sokoban, keeping XL 1 until the toplevel
Zoo monsters are generated as wimps, _then_ start
building your XPs, but probably, again, best done by
abandoning Sokoban as soon as the TopLevel is
generated, retreating to DLVL 1, and working your XL
up to snuff on easy DLVLs against easy monsters.

The _next_ time you get to the entrance to Sokoban
is soon enough to go clean out what will now be a
"walk in the park" Zoo with your newly earned much
bigger HP maximum, though the randomly generated
monsters as you go along will match your newfound
buffness.

[Umm, and don't go nuts on this whole process.
Keeping XL 1 isn't a law of nature or even a
conduct, it is a convenience to survival. If your
pet escapes your vicinity and the monsters corner
you, do Elbereth as possible, and _kill_ _something_
_quick_, _before_ your HPs are near zero.]

